<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part I &mdash; Foundations &middot; Chapter 1</p>
  <h1>Why Does Anything Exist?</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Not a deeper particle, not an earlier instant. Two boundary conditions, one constant
    of closure, and a half-turn that opens a dimension &mdash; and then matter, with nowhere else to go.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Begin Here</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-boundary-conditions">Two Boundary Conditions</h2>
    <p class="lede">Zero and one are not two small numbers. They are the two ends of measurability, and
      neither is reachable from inside.</p>

    <p><strong>Zero</strong> is the floor no shrinking quantity ever reaches. Halve a thing forever and you
      produce an endless sequence that converges on zero and never arrives. It is an infinity opening
      downward, into the small. <strong>One</strong> is the edge of the unit, the boundary of
      <em>this</em> against <em>that</em>, the threshold at which a thing is a thing at all.</p>

    <p>Between them sits the fact the whole framework is built on. The interval from zero to one contains
      more numbers than the entire unending march of 1, 2, 3, 4&hellip; put together.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> This is Cantor's diagonal result, rigorous and unarguable: the
      &ldquo;small&rdquo; infinity nested between the two markers is strictly denser than the
      &ldquo;large&rdquo; one that runs to the stars. The framework does not treat this as a curiosity. It
      treats it as a <strong>tilt built into the foundations</strong> &mdash; and much later, in
      <a href="/seams/#t-arrow">the chapter on the arrow</a>, it cashes that tilt in as the direction of
      time.</p>

    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established mathematics or physics</span>
      &middot; <span class="hist">&#9670; history and scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's own argument, contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">1.2 &mdash; The Constant of Closure</p>
    <h2 id="t-pi">π Is Not Found Inside the Universe. It Is the Condition of There Being One.</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Ask how zero and one are joined. Draw the path straight and it never closes: it
      runs on forever, resolving nothing, producing nothing finite that could stand and stay.</p>
  </div>

