<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the chain, read backwards</p>
    <h2 id="t-irreversible">Why the Chain Will Not Run the Other Way</h2>
    <p>A chain of necessity is only worth the name if it is directed. Six links that could be traversed in
      either order would be a list, not a derivation. So it is worth stating plainly what forbids the
      return journey at each joint.</p>
    <p>From α to π the step is <em>closure</em>: an interval acquires a second side. Closure destroys
      information about where on the interval you began, which is exactly why it cannot be undone &mdash;
      a circle does not remember its cut. From π to π/2 the step is <em>splitting</em>: one rotation becomes
      two independent half-rotations, and the imaginary axis appears. Splitting is not a loss but a
      promotion, and promotions are not reversible either; you cannot unsplit a plane back into a line
      without choosing which direction to discard, and nothing in the geometry makes that choice. From π/2
      to i the step is <em>orientation</em>, and orientation is the one thing a half-twist takes away
      permanently. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">Each link forgets something. That is what makes the chain a direction rather than a
      sequence, and it is the earliest appearance in this work of the arrow that
      <a href="/seams/#t-arrow">Part VI</a> has to account for.</p>
    <p class="muted">The honest reservation: &ldquo;cannot be undone&rdquo; is doing heavy lifting across
      three different senses &mdash; information loss, dimensional promotion, and loss of orientability.
      They are genuinely different kinds of irreversibility, and a finished version of this argument would
      prove that each one implies a thermodynamic direction rather than merely resembling it. It does
      not.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; from Chapter 23, <em>A Number Made of Itself</em></p>
    <h2 id="t-pi-made">What It Means to Say π Is Not Found Inside the Universe</h2>
    <p>The claim at the head of this chapter sounds mystical and is not. It is a claim about the order of
      dependence. A physical constant &mdash; the fine-structure constant, the mass of the electron &mdash;
      is a number you could only discover by being in a universe and looking. Change the universe and the
      number changes with it; it carries no authority outside the arrangement that produced it.</p>
    <p>π is not that kind of number. It is what you get when you ask what the ratio must be <em>for there to
      be a closed curve at all</em>. No measurement is involved and none could be: a universe with a
      different π is not a universe with different physics but a universe with no closure, and therefore no
      boundary, and therefore nothing that could be distinguished from anything else. π is a precondition
      masquerading as a quantity. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>This is also why the framework keeps returning to it rather than to the constants that look more
      impressive. A precondition can be reasoned about from the inside, which is the only place any of us
      is standing. A constant can only be measured, and measurement already presupposes the closure that π
      names. <a href="/essays/#t-number">The essay on this</a> puts it the short way: the one number the
      universe did not have to go and find.</p>
    <p class="muted">What is refused here: the slide from &ldquo;π is necessary&rdquo; to &ldquo;π is
      therefore causally responsible&rdquo;. Necessity is not agency. The framework says the geometry
      constrains what can exist; it does not say the geometry sat down and decided.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- expand:foundations:end -->

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing Stance</p>
    <p>The chapter does not claim that no other explanation of existence could work. It claims that this
      one requires no ingredients beyond two numbers and the constant that joins them &mdash; and that a
      theory asking for less is hard to imagine. Press it where it is weakest: at the word
      <em>must</em>.</p>
